Proof systems are systems used to prove things by a prover to a verifier. IOP is a quite new proof system, which is design to combine the IP and PCP probabilistic proof systems. In an IOP, the prover and the verifier has a communication phase in which they interact for number of rounds. Since it was created, many uses for it have been discovered. It is still a  quite new object and so there are many research directions on this object. One of those directions is the reductions of the round complexity of an IOP. For example, by general transformation for IOPs that given some IOP outputs new IOP for the same problem with reduced number of rounds.
    
        Another important property of IOP (and proof system in general) is the ZK property, for cases in which we want to save some secret that the prover has from the verifier.
        The goal of this work is to examine the effect of transformations for rounds reduction on the ZK property.
        We first looked at the reduction presented at[[ABCY22]](https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/790.pdf) and proved that it is preserved ZK, but is not always relevant and effective. Then we looked at the reduction represented at [[BM88]](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022000088900281) for IPs. We adjusted it to IOPs and proved correctness and HVZK preservation under reasonable assumption.